#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e290e8b2-7766-55ff-95da-99a3430b18e3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Jacobian of an Inverse Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd1b8fb-a982-5388-a199-722a82b95b93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294568} {:answer/id #uuid "06c836e0-229b-580d-a602-e7e8c5e81ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294569} {:answer/id #uuid "fc94578a-29bd-50bd-865f-8a07e4caba95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294570} {:answer/id #uuid "3a303d05-743b-5ed4-889e-6ba1e4dcc85e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294571} {:answer/id #uuid "4495adaa-78ff-5e82-90ca-fa8312422455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4495adaa-78ff-5e82-90ca-fa8312422455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294572} :answer-field/id #uuid "d096282b-f341-56d8-b0da-efc1326e9112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5372554f-c783-5b2a-8ab6-cd073e081874" :question/math-academy-id "q-161798" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T:(u,v,w)\\to (x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w)),$ given that \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=z,\\,w=4y-2{z}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're only given ${T}^{-1}.$ So, we compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)},$ the Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}.$\n\n- Then, we will compute the Jacobian determinant of $T$ using the relation\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂y} & =1 & \\,\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂v}{∂z} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =4 & \\,\\frac{∂w}{∂z} & =-4z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant of ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4z\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(0-4) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & ={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
